# What happens when the government caps rents?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does the supply and demand model predict for a price ceiling? What did San Francisco's rent control actually do to tenants and to supply?"
next: {"title":"What happens when the government sets a minimum price?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/19-what-happens-when-the-government-sets-a.cast"}

## A cap that raised rents
sources:
    - id: dmq2019
      title: 'The Effects of Rent Control Expansion on Tenants, Landlords, and Inequality: Evidence from San Francisco'
      authors: Rebecca Diamond, Tim McQuade, Franklin Qian
      year: 2019
      finding: 'San Francisco''s November 1994 ballot measure brought small multifamily buildings built before 1980 under rent control. Relative to buildings built 1980-1990, the number of renters living in treated buildings fell 15% (landlords converted to condos/TICs, sold to owner-occupiers or redeveloped); the authors estimate the supply cut raised city-wide rents by 5.1%. American Economic Review 109(9): 3365-3394; NBER WP 24181.'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.20181289

    icon old size 230 set twemoji x 290 y 480 icon_look picture of house hidden true
    icon new size 230 set twemoji x 710 y 480 icon_look picture of house hidden true
    text old_l "built 1979" x 290 y 345 font_size 32 hidden true
    text new_l "built 1981" x 710 y 345 font_size 32 hidden true
    path divide dashed #8f887c points [[500,350],[500,590]] hidden true
    text vote "Nov 1994: cap on rent rises" #b5482e x 500 y 625 font_size 32 hidden true
    icon lock size 80 set twemoji x 390 y 560 icon_look picture of locked hidden true

    card title "A cap that raised rents"

What happens when the government caps rents? Most people's first answer is simple: tenants pay less, landlords earn less, and that's it.
    draw old new

B: That's my answer too. It's a transfer: money moves from landlords to tenants.
    text naive "landlords → tenants ?" #8f887c x 500 y 250 font_size 36

Let's test it o (@erase naive@)n a real case. San Francisco has limited yearly rent rises since 1979, but small buildings, with four flats or fewer, were left out.
    draw old_l new_l

Then, in November 1994, the voters closed t (@draw vote@)hat gap: small buildings built before 1980 came un (@draw lock@)der the cap. Those built from 1980 on stayed free.
    draw divide

B: So two near-identical buildings, a few years apart in age, and only one gets capped. That's almost an experiment.
    highlight old new effect pulse

It is, and three Stanford economists, Rebecca Diamond, Tim McQuade and Franklin Qian, followed thousands of those tenants for years.
    scale renters bottom cites ["dmq2019"] label "Change in renters living there" unit % min -30 max 30 value -15 ticks 6

    erase vote

    ask "How did the number of renters in the capped buildings change, against their uncapped twins? Click the line." intro "Your turn, then." on renters store g voice b
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it was {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was {g.true}. A guess is a fine place to start.

It fell by fifteen percent. Landlords sold flats to owners, turned them into condominiums, or tore the building down and built new, and new buildings were exempt.
    highlight renters_answer effect pulse

B: Fewer flats to rent, in a city already short of them.
    fade old old_l lock to 0.4

And the authors estimate that this pushed rents across the whole city up by about five percent. A cap on rents that raised rents.
    text rents "City-wide rents: +5.1%" #b5482e cites ["dmq2019"] x 710 y 642 font_size 36

To see how that can happen, we need the market diagram, with a lid on it.
    highlight rents effect underline

## A lid on the market
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Flats (Q)
    y_label: Rent per month (P)
    demand: {}
    supply:
      elasticity: 0.5
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: $1000
      q_label: '500'
    price_ceiling:
      level: 32
      label: Rent cap $640
    regions:
      - transfer
      - deadweight_loss
    units:
      price: [0, 2000]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: flats
    readout:
      - shortage
    readout_at: inside

    card title "A lid on the market"

B: A cap that raises rents sounds like a paradox. On the diagram it is almost the first thing you see.
    draw axes

B: Take Harbourtown's rental flats, with illustrative numbers. Tenants' demand, landlords' supply, and t (@draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar@)he market rent where they cross: about a thousand a month, for five hundred flats.
    draw demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S

Now the harbour council says no flat may cost more than, say, si (@fade guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar to 0.35@)x hundred and forty a month.
    draw ceiling_line label_ceiling

B: At that rent, more people want a flat of their own: that's Qd.
    draw shortage_guide_qd label_Qd

B: But landlords offer fewer: that's Qs. The gap (@draw shortage_arrow label_shortage readout@) between them is the shortage.
    draw shortage_guide_qs label_Qs

So the tenants who do have a flat pay less. That part of the first answer was right.
    highlight ceiling_line

B: It was. This rectangle moves from landlords to sitting tenants.
    draw transfer_region label_transfer

B: But this triangle is simply gone: flats someone would gladly rent, and someone would gladly let, now never get let.
    draw dwl_region label_DWL

And the flats that remain go to whoever got there first, or never moves, not to whoever needs them most.
    highlight shortage_guide_qs label_Qs

B: Now give it time. A landlord can't remove a building overnight, but over years they sell, convert or build something else, so supply grows more responsive.
    highlight supply_curve

The same cap, and the shortage grows: the lid bites har (@highlight shortage_arrow label_shortage@)der the longer it stays.
    animate supply.elasticity 1 duration 3

B: Try it yourself: drag the rent cap down, then up past the market rent, and watch the shortage open and vanish.
    explore

## Who paid in San Francisco
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 'Gain: capped tenants'
      - 'Cost: higher rents for all'
    values:
      - 2.9
      - 2.9
    value_labels: true
    colors:
      - green
      - red
    y_label: $ billion (present value)
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 5
    title: San Francisco, 1995–2012
    box: left
    label_size: 30
sources:
    - id: dmq2019
      title: 'The Effects of Rent Control Expansion on Tenants, Landlords, and Inequality: Evidence from San Francisco'
      authors: Rebecca Diamond, Tim McQuade, Franklin Qian
      year: 2019
      finding: 'Tenants in buildings brought under San Francisco rent control in 1994 were 10-20% more likely to stay at their 1994 address, more so for older and long-tenure households; their gains over 1995-2012 total about $2.9 billion in present value. Landlords'' supply cuts raised city-wide rents 5.1%, a present-value cost to renters of about $2.9 billion, 42% of it borne by people who did not yet live in San Francisco. American Economic Review 109(9): 3365-3394.'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.20181289

    scratch stay right work ["Capped tenants:","10–20% more","likely to stay"] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch later right cites ["dmq2019"] work ["42% of the cost:","people who came","later"] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Who paid in San Francisco"

The diagram says a cap helps the tenants who have a flat, and hurts those still looking. San Francisco lets us count both.
    draw axes title

B: Start with the winners. Tenants in the newly capped buildings were ten to twenty percent more likely to still live at their address years later.
    draw stay_box stay_line_1 stay_line_2 stay_line_3

And older tenants, and those who had lived there longest, gained the most: they could stay near their friends, their doctor, their work.
    highlight stay_line_2 stay_line_3

    erase stay

B: Added up over 1995 to 2012, the authors put the gain to those tenants at about two point nine billion dollars, in present value.
    draw bar_1

    ask "How big was the cost to all renters of the higher rents? Drag the bar to your guess." on bar_2 store c relative true
        intro: Now the losers: everyone who paid the higher city-wide rents that the shrinking supply caused.
        right: Close: you said {c} billion, and it was {c.true}.
        wrong: You said {c} billion; it was {c.true}.

B: About two point nine billion as well. The same size as the gain.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

So by their estimate, the cap mostly moved money between renters: from everyone else to the ones lucky enough to sit in a capped flat in 1994.
    highlight bar_2

B: And forty-two percent of that cost fell on people who didn't live in San Francisco yet. They never got a vote.
    draw later_box later_line_1 later_line_2 later_line_3

The textbook shortage was there, just not as a queue outside one door: it showed up as fewer flats to rent and higher rents everywhere else.
    highlight bar_2 effect box

## A lid, or insurance?
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Flats (Q)
    y_label: Rent per month (P)
    demand: {}
    supply:
      elasticity: 0.5
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: $1000
      q_label: '500'
    price_ceiling:
      level: 32
      label: Rent cap $640
    regions: []
    units:
      price: [0, 2000]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: flats
sources:
    - id: dmq2019
      title: 'The Effects of Rent Control Expansion on Tenants, Landlords, and Inequality: Evidence from San Francisco'
      authors: Rebecca Diamond, Tim McQuade, Franklin Qian
      year: 2019
      finding: 'Under San Francisco''s rent control the landlord may reset the rent when a tenant leaves, and newer buildings are exempt; landlords responded by converting and redeveloping, cutting rental supply. The authors suggest that insurance against large rent increases provided as government social insurance, rather than as a mandate on landlords, could avoid much of the welfare loss. AER 109(9): 3365-3394.'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.20181289
    - id: igm2012
      title: Rent Control (US Economic Experts Panel poll)
      authors: IGM Forum / Clark Center, University of Chicago Booth
      year: 2012
      finding: '7 February 2012: on the statement that local rent-control ordinances in cities such as New York and San Francisco had a positive effect on the amount and quality of broadly affordable rental housing, 1 of 37 responding panelists agreed, 3 were uncertain and 33 disagreed.'
      url: https://www.kentclarkcenter.org/surveys/rent-control/

    scratch design top-right cites ["dmq2019"] work ["Rent resets when","a tenant leaves","New buildings exempt","= insurance against","rent jumps"] font_size 34 hidden true
    scratch sure top-right cites ["igm2012"] work ["Supply shrinks:","mostly agreed","Better design:","open"] font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch math top-right work [{"tex":"Q_d = a - bP \\quad Q_s = c + dP"},{"tex":"\\text{Shortage} = Q_d(P_c) - Q_s(P_c)"},{"tex":"= (b+d)\\,(P^* - P_c)"},"Long run: d ↑ → shortage ↑"] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "A lid, or insurance?"

B: So far the cap is a lid on the whole market. Is th (@fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar ceiling_line label_ceiling shortage_guides label_Qd label_Qs shortage_arrow label_shortage to 0.3@)at really what San Francisco has?
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar ceiling_line label_ceiling shortage_guides label_Qd label_Qs shortage_arrow label_shortage

Not quite. It limits how fast a sitting tenant's rent can rise.
    draw design_box

When the tenant moves out, the landlord can reset the rent, and new buildings are exempt.
    draw design_line_1 design_line_2 design_line_3

B: Then it's less a lid than an insurance policy: if rents in the city soar, you aren't priced out of your home.
    draw design_line_4 design_line_5

And that insurance is worth something real: staying near your friends, your school, your job. The simple diagram has no place for it.
    highlight design_line_4 design_line_5

B: Then why do economists dislike it so much?
    highlight shortage_arrow

Because the landlord pays for the insurance, so the landlord has a reason to get out of renting. Diamond and her co-authors suggest the government could provide that insurance instead.
    highlight supply_curve

B: How sure are we, then?
    erase design

That caps shrink rental supply is mostly agreed: in a 2012 poll, of thirty-seven leading US economists who answered, only one agreed that rent control had helped affordable housing.
    draw sure_box sure_line_1 sure_line_2

Whether a softer design can keep the stability without the shrinkage is still open.
    draw sure_line_3 sure_line_4

    erase sure

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own

@math

    hide mathq

Write demand and supply as straight lines.
    draw math_box math_line_1

At the capped rent, the shortage is the quantity demanded minus the quantity supplied.
    draw math_line_2

That works out to b plus d, times how far the cap sits below the market rent.
    draw math_line_3

As supply grows more responsive over the years, d grows, and so does the shortage.
    draw math_line_4

    erase math

@test

    hide mathq

    quiz "Harbourtown's council caps the fee for a harbour mooring at half the market fee. What follows?" voice b
        right: At half the fee more boat owners want a mooring and fewer are offered, so a queue forms.
        wrong: At half the fee, do more boat owners want a mooring, and do more owners offer one?
        + A waiting list for moorings
        * More moorings offered
        * No change at all

    quiz "A city caps rents at $3,000 a month where the market rent is $2,000. What happens?"
        right: A cap above the market rent does not bind: rents stay where supply and demand put them.
        wrong: Does a lid above the market rent stop anyone doing what they would do anyway?
        * A shortage of flats
        + Very little
        * Rents fall to $2,000

    quiz "A hard rent cap stays in place for ten years. Compared with year one, the shortage is…" voice b
        right: Over ten years landlords sell, convert or never build, so supply responds more and the shortage grows.
        wrong: A landlord can't change a building overnight. What about in ten years?
        + Larger
        * Smaller
        * The same

    quiz "Which design protects sitting tenants while giving landlords the least reason to stop renting?"
        right: If the government carries the insurance, renting out a flat pays as before, so landlords have no new reason to leave.
        wrong: In each design, who pays for the protection, and does that payer then want out?
        + Government pays part of a sitting tenant's large rent rise
        * A hard cap on every rent
        * The cap extended to new buildings

So a rent cap does help the tenants who stay, and it pays for that with flats that are never let.
    fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar ceiling_line label_ceiling shortage_guides label_Qd label_Qs shortage_arrow label_shortage to 1

Ask that of any price set by law: who gets the good now, and who will find there is none left?
    highlight shortage_arrow label_shortage

